#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111756} :activity/id #uuid "13671180-c23c-5b99-86c7-3b830ac09165" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048201 :topic/id #uuid "87ab6c72-c0cd-50e6-96db-1aee2ca30ad9" :topic/math-academy-id 1458} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111843 :tutorial/content "Consider the following triangle, where the side $\\overline{AC}$ has been extended beyond the vertex $C$.\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\nThe resulting angle $\\angle{BCD}$ is called the **exterior angle** at vertex $C$.\n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n$\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{BCD}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{ACB}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} = 180^\\circ$\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + m∠C|= 180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math])$.\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently they have the same measure.![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d29b69d0-268e-57ed-9c22-289173b3c810" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2223 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dfd89506-bf95-546f-9871-fdf496b55589" :step/math-academy-id 9126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110400 :question/id #uuid "1642f15d-32c8-5a19-b20b-95f501dc0a97" :question/math-academy-id "e-2711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049949}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111254}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles"} :step/id #uuid "5b8b06f5-4b02-515a-b65f-2c8a74aeac87" :step/math-academy-id 9129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111844 :tutorial/content "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\n> The sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have\n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= m∠B + m∠C \\\\\ny &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\nz &= m∠A + m∠B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= (m∠B + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠B) \\\\\n&= 2m∠A + 2m∠B + 2m∠C \\\\\n&= 2(m∠A + m∠B + m∠C) \\\\\n&= 2(180^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f605c56-a279-51cc-be43-72f1b9030f58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2226 :tutorial/title "The Sum of Exterior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "22624b6a-7b76-5955-88d7-2768f50c463e" :step/math-academy-id 9130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110401 :question/id #uuid "d9bf29fd-5151-591f-b6af-8f5e53b3d231" :question/math-academy-id "e-2712"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba344d2f-aeb4-54ca-ab5d-e0abeceddba5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111259}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given the Exterior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "0cffc650-bf34-5697-9746-2b68e2f3da89" :step/math-academy-id 9132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110402 :question/id #uuid "e97d1ab9-f0b2-5e27-afff-a84da69c7487" :question/math-academy-id "e-2714"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66f215f1-b90c-5284-8628-6a94bf37de0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111265}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Interior Angle Given the Exterior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "81bb680b-a34b-5ad8-9b3f-f51aa2a0acc1" :step/math-academy-id 9134}] :activity/title "Exterior Angles of Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110830}]}
